            <!-- Section 1: What is ITSM -->
            <section class="content-section" id="what-is-itsm">
                <h2>What is Modern ITSM?</h2>
                <p>
                    <strong>ServiceNow IT Service Management (ITSM)</strong> is the platform's flagship application suite, 
                    designed to design, deliver, manage, and optimize the entire lifecycle of Information Technology services 
                    across an enterprise.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Rather than operating in fragmented email inboxes and disparate point tools, ServiceNow consolidates 
                    IT infrastructure operations, service desks, engineering squads, and end users onto a single 
                    cloud-native data model rooted in the <strong>Common Service Data Model (CSDM)</strong>.
                </p>

                <div class="info-card info-card--important">
                    <div class="info-card-title">Core Mental Model</div>
                    <p>
                        ITSM is the <strong>operational nervous system</strong> of the enterprise. While the CMDB tracks 
                        <em>what exists</em> (servers, databases, services), ITSM manages <em>what is happening</em> to those assets — 
                        restoring broken services (Incident), finding root causes (Problem), governing modifications (Change), 
                        fulfilling user needs (Request), and documenting institutional intelligence (Knowledge).
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 2: ITIL v4 Alignment -->
            <section class="content-section" id="itil-alignment">
                <h2>ITIL v4 & Service Value System</h2>
                <p>
                    ServiceNow ITSM is fully certified against the <strong>ITIL v4 (Information Technology Infrastructure Library)</strong> 
                    framework. ITIL v4 shifts the focus from rigid, siloed process steps to an adaptive <strong>Service Value System (SVS)</strong> 
                    and flexible <strong>Value Streams</strong>.
                </p>

                <div class="tabs-container">
                    <div class="tabs-header">
                        <button class="tab-btn active" data-tab="tab-guiding-principles">Guiding Principles</button>
                        <button class="tab-btn" data-tab="tab-four-dimensions">4 Dimensions</button>
                        <button class="tab-btn" data-tab="tab-value-chain">Service Value Chain</button>
                    </div>

                    <div class="tab-panel active" id="tab-guiding-principles">
                        <h3>The 7 ITIL Guiding Principles in ServiceNow</h3>
                        <ul class="feature-bullet-list">
                            <li><strong>Focus on Value:</strong> Every incident resolved and request fulfilled directly maps to business service availability.</li>
                            <li><strong>Start Where You Are:</strong> Ingest existing configuration items via Discovery instead of rebuilding from scratch.</li>
                            <li><strong>Progress Iteratively with Feedback:</strong> Use CSAT surveys and Continual Improvement Initiatives (`sn_cim_register`).</li>
                            <li><strong>Collaborate & Promote Visibility:</strong> Major Incident Workbench connects resolvers, executives, and communications teams in real time.</li>
                            <li><strong>Think & Work Holistically:</strong> Cross-module integration ensures changes link to impacted CIs, outages, and downstream services.</li>
                            <li><strong>Keep It Simple & Practical:</strong> Standard Change templates and Record Producers eliminate administrative friction.</li>
                            <li><strong>Optimize & Automate:</strong> Flow Designer and Now Assist GenAI eliminate manual ticket routing and resolution summaries.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>

                    <div class="tab-panel" id="tab-four-dimensions">
                        <h3>The Four Dimensions of Service Management</h3>
                        <p>To ensure balanced service delivery, ServiceNow structures ITSM across four architectural dimensions:</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li><strong>Organizations & People:</strong> Defined via User Profiles (`sys_user`), Assignment Groups (`sys_user_group`), and Skills.</li>
                            <li><strong>Information & Technology:</strong> The CMDB (`cmdb_ci`), Service Mapping, and the Knowledge Base (`kb_knowledge`).</li>
                            <li><strong>Partners & Suppliers:</strong> Vendor performance tracking, Underpinning Contracts (UCs), and external integrations.</li>
                            <li><strong>Value Streams & Processes:</strong> End-to-end automated flows from Incident triage to Request fulfillment.</li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>

                    <div class="tab-panel" id="tab-value-chain">
                        <h3>The Service Value Chain Activities</h3>
                        <p>ServiceNow native workflows operationalize the six core value chain activities:</p>
                        <table class="data-table">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Activity</th>
                                    <th>ServiceNow ITSM Realization</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Plan</strong></td>
                                    <td>Continual Improvement Management (CIM), Release planning, CAB schedules.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Improve</strong></td>
                                    <td>Problem Root Cause Analysis (RCA), Knowledge generation (KCS), SLA repair.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Engage</strong></td>
                                    <td>Employee Center, Service Operations Workspace (SOW), Omnichannel Chat/Walk-up.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Design & Transition</strong></td>
                                    <td>Change Models, Risk Assessments, Catalog Item design, CI Dependency mapping.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Obtain / Build</strong></td>
                                    <td>Procurement catalog tasks, Integration Hub spokes, asset allocation.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Deliver & Support</strong></td>
                                    <td>Incident Management, Major Incident conference bridges, Task SLAs.</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 3: Platform Architecture Layers -->
            <section class="content-section" id="ecosystem-architecture">
                <h2>Platform Architecture Layers</h2>
                <p>
                    ServiceNow ITSM is built on a 4-tier platform architecture that isolates user presentation from 
                    underlying transactional data integrity:
                </p>

                <div class="diagram-block">
                    <div class="diagram-title">ServiceNow ITSM 4-Tier Platform Architecture</div>
                    <div class="mermaid">
                    graph TB
                        subgraph ExperienceLayer["1. Experience & Interaction Layer"]
                            EC[Employee Center Portal]
                            SOW[Service Operations Workspace]
                            VA[Virtual Agent & Now Assist GenAI]
                            WK[Walk-up Kiosk & Tech Bar]
                        end

                        subgraph ProcessLayer["2. Process Automation & Business Logic"]
                            FD[Flow Designer & Subflows]
                            BR[Business Rules & State Handlers]
                            DL[Data Lookup Rules: Priority / Assignment]
                            SLAE[SLA Engine & Condition Rules]
                        end

                        subgraph DataLayer["3. Relational Data Model & CMDB"]
                            TASK[task Super-Table Hierarchy]
                            INC_TAB[incident / incident_task]
                            PRB_TAB[problem / problem_task]
                            CHG_TAB[change_request / change_task]
                            REQ_TAB[sc_request / sc_req_item / sc_task]
                            CSDM_TAB[CSDM 4.0: Services & CIs]
                        end

                        subgraph IntegrationLayer["4. Integration & Connectivity Layer"]
                            IH[Integration Hub Spokes: Azure / AWS / Jira]
                            REST[Scripted REST & Table APIs]
                            SGC[Service Graph Connectors: SCCM / Intune]
                        end

                        ExperienceLayer --> ProcessLayer
                        ProcessLayer --> DataLayer
                        IntegrationLayer <--> DataLayer
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 4: Core Process Modules -->
            <section class="content-section" id="core-modules">
                <h2>Core Process Modules</h2>
                <p>The ITSM suite is organized into 6 tightly connected operational domains:</p>

                <div class="feature-grid">
                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Incident Management</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>Rapid restoration of normal service operations with minimal disruption. Includes P1/P2 Major Incident Management (MIM) workbenches and automated on-call escalations.</p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Problem Management</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>Identifies the underlying root causes of recurring incidents. Structures Root Cause Analysis (RCA), publishes Known Error workarounds, and initiates permanent change requests.</p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Change & Release</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>Governs modifications to IT infrastructure and business applications using modern Change Models (Normal, Standard, Emergency, Unauthorized) and CAB Workbench automation.</p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Service Catalog & Requests</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>Provides an intuitive shopping experience for business goods and IT services. Implements the 3-tier Request model (<code>sc_request</code> &gt; <code>sc_req_item</code> &gt; <code>sc_task</code>) with Flow Designer approvals.</p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Knowledge Management</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>Centralizes organizational knowledge and self-help articles. Enforces granular User Criteria security and enables Knowledge-Centered Service (KCS) generation directly from resolved tickets.</p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Service Level Management</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>Monitors service commitments across SLAs, internal OLAs, and vendor Underpinning Contracts (UCs). Features retroactive start timers, schedule calculations, and breach notifications.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 5: RBAC Roles -->
            <section class="content-section" id="rbac-roles">
                <h2>Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)</h2>
                <p>
                    Security and operational permissions in ServiceNow ITSM follow strict role inheritance patterns:
                </p>

                <table class="data-table">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Role Name</th>
                            <th>Target Persona</th>
                            <th>Inherited Rights & Permissions</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>itil</code></td>
                            <td>Service Desk Agents & Technical Engineers</td>
                            <td>Create, read, update incidents, problems, changes, and service requests. Base fulfilling technician role.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>itil_admin</code></td>
                            <td>ITSM Team Leads & Administrators</td>
                            <td>Full administrative authority over ITSM records, override closed states, delete records, and modify assignment rules.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>incident_manager</code></td>
                            <td>Incident Process Owner / MIM Manager</td>
                            <td>Propose and accept Major Incidents, manage incident alerts, launch conference bridges, and sign off on PIRs.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>problem_manager</code></td>
                            <td>Problem Process Owner</td>
                            <td>Authorize problem root causes, approve risk acceptance, oversee Known Error publication, and close problems.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>change_manager</code></td>
                            <td>Change Process Owner / CAB Chair</td>
                            <td>Manage CAB meetings, approve Normal/Emergency changes, schedule changes, and resolve conflict detections.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>catalog_admin</code></td>
                            <td>Catalog Managers</td>
                            <td>Build and configure Catalog Items, Order Guides, Variable Sets, User Criteria, and Category hierarchies.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>knowledge_admin</code></td>
                            <td>Knowledge Process Owner</td>
                            <td>Administer Knowledge Bases, define approval workflows, configure User Criteria, and publish/retire articles.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>sla_admin</code></td>
                            <td>Service Level Managers</td>
                            <td>Create and maintain SLA Definitions (`contract_sla`), schedules (`cmn_schedule`), and condition scripts.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>snc_internal</code></td>
                            <td>All Internal Employees / Requesters</td>
                            <td>Base platform authentication role required to view Employee Center, submit requests, and log incidents.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 6: End-to-End Service Lifecycle -->
            <section class="content-section" id="lifecycle-flow">
                <h2>End-to-End Service Lifecycle</h2>
                <p>
                    The following sequence diagram illustrates how an initial end-user disruption orchestrates across 
                    every ITSM process module and the CMDB in an enterprise environment:
                </p>

                <div class="diagram-block">
                    <div class="diagram-title">ITSM Enterprise Cross-Module Execution Flow</div>
                    <div class="mermaid">
                    sequenceDiagram
                        autonumber
                        actor User as End User (Employee Center)
                        participant SOW as Service Desk Agent (SOW)
                        participant INC as Incident Management
                        participant PRB as Problem Management
                        participant KB as Knowledge Base (KCS)
                        participant CHG as Change Management (CAB)
                        participant REQ as Service Catalog (Fulfillment)

                        User->>INC: Logs Incident (VPN Gateway Unresponsive)
                        INC->>INC: Evaluates Priority (Impact 1 x Urgency 1 = P1 Critical)
                        INC->>SOW: Routes to Network Resolver via AWA
                        SOW->>INC: Resolves Immediate Disruption via Failover
                        INC->>User: Service Restored (Auto-close after 5 days)

                        INC->>PRB: Escalates to Problem for Root Cause Investigation
                        PRB->>PRB: RCA Identifies Firmware Memory Leak
                        PRB->>KB: Publishes Known Error Workaround Article
                        PRB->>CHG: Submits Emergency Change Request (RFC) for Firmware Patch

                        CHG->>CHG: Calculates Risk & Checks Maintenance Blackout Window
                        CHG->>CHG: CAB Approves Change Request
                        CHG->>CHG: Deploys Firmware Patch & Validates Health
                        CHG->>PRB: Marks Problem Permanently Solved

                        PRB->>REQ: Triggers Hardware Upgrade Request (Next-Gen Gateway)
                        REQ->>REQ: Procures, Images & Deploys Hardware
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

        </div>
